        <div id="third-party" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is Trader Joe's Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No third-party testing is published for any protein powder Trader Joe's sells. Neither the Orgain Protein Boost listing nor the Hydrolyzed Collagen page mentions NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport, Informed Choice or a Clean Label Project result. The Orgain tub carries the USDA Organic seal, but organic certification covers farming and processing rules, not contaminants or label accuracy.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not mean the powders are unsafe. It means you are relying on the manufacturer's own quality control and on general FDA rules for dietary supplements and foods, which do not require pre-market testing. Trader Joe's does not publish lot-level test results for these products, and Orgain does not publish a sport certification for its organic protein line either.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant proteins draw particular attention for heavy metals, because pea, rice and other crops take up lead and cadmium from soil. Independent testing groups have found measurable levels in many plant powders, including well-known brands, usually well within legal limits but higher than typical whey. If that worries you, our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> lists brands that publish results.
          </p>

          <p>
            Who needs a certified product? Competitive athletes subject to drug testing should buy only powders with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, and nothing at Trader Joe's qualifies. For everyone else, the bigger risk with an untested powder is a label that overstates protein, which is why we always work out cost from the label figures and recommend sticking with established manufacturers. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> goes into more detail.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Organic is not the same as tested.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A USDA Organic seal tells you how the ingredients were grown. NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport tells you the finished powder was checked for banned substances. If you are drug-tested, only the second counts.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

